%I A382922 #40 Sep 03 2026 10:17:00
%S A382922 31,77,231,354,523,535,631,819,827,830,991,1234
%N A382922 Numbers k such that Fibonacci(k) is a Smith number.
%C A382922 1440, 1554, 1596, 1863, 2256, 2277, and 2559 are also terms. If none of F_n, for n = 1423, 1427, 1429, 1433, is a Smith number, then 1440 is indeed the next term. - _Ronie P. Dario_, Aug 20 2026
%H A382922 Ronie P. Dario, João L. Gonçalves, and Moniky P. N. Oliveira, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01955">When Fibonacci and Lucas Meet Smith: A Computational Exploration</a>, arXiv:2609.01955 [math.NT], 2026.
%H A382922 Shyam Sunder Gupta, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-97-2465-9_4">Smith Numbers</a>, Exploring the Beauty of Fascinating Numbers, Springer (2025) Ch. 4, 127-157.
%e A382922 31 is a term since Fibonacci(31) = 1346269 = 557 * 2417, and 1346269 is a Smith number (1 + 3 + 4 + 6 + 2 + 6 + 9 = 5 + 5 + 7 + 2 + 4 + 1 + 7).
%Y A382922 Cf. A000045, A006753.
